ace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}9\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3.68 + 5.42 \\approx 9\n$$"] [17592186142588 :question/worked-solution "To estimate the amount of fruit juice that Maria prepared, we have to find an approximation for the sum\n$0.73 + 4.18$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest benchmark:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}0.73 & + & 4.18 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{0.5}} & + & {\\color{blue}{4.0}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  \\\\ \\hline  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Maria prepared approximately $4.5$ liters of fruit juice."] [17592186144028 :tutorial/content "Sometimes when multiplying the ones, the result is two digits long.\n\nIn this case, we write down the digit in the ones place, and we **carry** the digit in the tens place. We then add the carried digit to the result of the multiplication of the tens.\n\nFor example, let's multiply $26$ by $6$.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rlr}\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{cccc}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 6 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\times  \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 6 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} \\phantom{14 \\hspace{1em} \\text{and} \\hspace{1em} {\\color{blue}3} + 14 = 170000}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones. We have\n\n$$\n6\\times6= {\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}6}\n$$\n\n, so we write the ones place value, $\\color{red}6$, and carry the tens place value, ${\\color{blue}3}$:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rlrl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\begin{array}{cccc}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\underset{ {\\color{blue}3}}{} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\bbox[2px,lightgray]{6} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\times  \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\bbox[2px,lightgray]{6} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{red}6 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}}\\end{array} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle \\begin{array}{c}\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px,lightgray]{6} \\times  \\bbox[2px,lightgray]{6} = 36 \\phantom{00000000000000000} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\text{Carry:} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\color{blue}3} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\text{Write:} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\color{red}6}\\end{array}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the tens. We have\n\n$$\n2\\times 6 = 12\n$$\n\n, to which we add the carry of ${\\color{blue}3}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{array}{cccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{}_{{\\color{blue}{3}}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  \\\\  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\times \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{red}{1\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}}} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{red}{5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}}} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array} & \\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em} & \\begin{aligned}2\\times 6=12\\hspace{1em}\\text{and}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{blue}{3}}+12=15 \\\\ \\text{Carry:}\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}{-}} \\\\ \\text{Write:}\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{red}{15}}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen we reach the tens multiplication, we do not carry anything. Instead, we write down the complete result.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n26 \\times 6 = 156\n$$"] [17592186144030 :tutorial/content "We can extend our understanding of place value to multiply three-digit numbers by one-digit numbers.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n145 \\times {\\color{red}{4}}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's write the three-digit number $(145)$ in expanded form:\n\n$$\n145 = {\\color{blue}{100}} + {\\color{blue}{40}} + {\\color{blue}{5}}\n$$\n\nWe now multiply all numbers in our expanded form by ${\\color{red}{4}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n100 \\cdot 4 &= 400 \\\\\n40 \\cdot 4 &= 160 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 4 &= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now add these results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccccc} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}1\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}8\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n145 \\times 4 = 580\n$$"] [17592186144031 :tutorial/content "We can use multiplication as repeated addition and the relationship between multiplication and division to solve division problems.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n20 \\times 4 = {\\color{blue}{80}}\n$$\n\nAs we've seen, we can generate more multiplication problems using repeated addition. For example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n21 \\cdot 4 &= 80 + 4 = 84 \\\\\n22 \\cdot 4 &= 84 + 4 = 88\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's write these results a little more clearly (without the intermediate step):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n21 \\cdot 4 &= 84 \\\\\n22 \\cdot 4 &= 88\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEach of these new multiplication problems can be used to generate *two* new division problems!\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\color{red}{84}} & ÷4=21,\\hspace{2em} &  & {\\color{red}{84}}÷21=4 \\\\ {\\color{purple}{88}} & ÷4=22,\\hspace{2em} &  & {\\color{purple}{88}}÷22=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use this idea to help us divide large numbers. Let's see how."] [17592186144032 :tutorial/content "We can also use repeated *subtraction* and the relationship between multiplication and division to solve division problems.\n\nWe once again consider the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n20 \\times 4 = {\\color{blue}{80}}\n$$\n\nLet's generate some more multiplication problems using repeated subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n19 \\cdot 4 &= 80 - 4 = 76 \\\\\n18 \\cdot 4 &= 76 - 4 = 72\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting these results a little more clearly (without the intermediate step), we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n19 \\cdot 4 &= 76 \\\\\n18 \\cdot 4 &= 72\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEach of these new multiplication problems can be used to generate two new division problems:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\color{red}{76}} & ÷4=19,\\hspace{2em} &  & {\\color{red}{76}}÷19=4 \\\\ {\\color{purple}{72}} & ÷4=18,\\hspace{2em} &  & {\\color{purple}{72}}÷18=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now use repeated subtraction to help us divide some large numbers."] [17592186144098 :tutorial/content "We can estimate the sum of two decimals by rounding each decimal to the nearest benchmark before adding.\n\nFor instance, let's use benchmarks to estimate the following sum:\n\n$$\n4.62 + 1.97\n$$\n\nFirst, we round each number to the nearest benchmark:\n\n- The number $\\color{blue}4.62$ falls between the benchmarks $4.5$ and $5.0$. The point that lies halfway between our two benchmarks is ${\\color{red}4.75}$. Since $4.62$ is closest to $4.5$, this is the number we round to.![](images/85/85d13fddf4e79494aafeb4be12a281c9432281c37ff12ffedd76542c49c7a499.png)\n- The number $\\color{blue}1.97$ falls between the benchmarks $1.5$ and $2.0$. The point that lies halfway between our two benchmarks is ${\\color{red}1.75}$. Since $1.97$ is closest to $2.0$, this is the number we round to.![](images/16/16d3360f953cbe22a0528b7f9023f5c700792af3882bbb50342a23f34ca68a4e.png)\n\nSo, rounding both numbers to the nearest benchmark, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}4.62 & + & 1.97 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{4.5}} & + & {\\color{blue}{2.0}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4.62 + 1.97$ is approximately $6.5$. We can express this fact using an \"is approximately equal to\" symbol\n\n$$\n(\\approx)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n4.62 + 1.97\\approx 6.5\n$$"] [17592186147180 :question/worked-solution "We are given that the mean is ${\\color{blue}5}$.\n\nSo, we subtract the mean from each number in the data set, find the absolute values of the results, and add all these together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|9-{\\color{blue}{5}}|+|2-{\\color{blue}{5}}|+|3-{\\color{blue}{5}}|+|6-{\\color{blue}{5}}| &= |4|+|-3|+|-2|+|1| \\\\ &= 4+3+2+1 \\\\ &= {\\color{red}{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the mean absolute deviation (MAD), we divide the sum obtained above (${\\color{red}10}$) by the total number of data points ($4$):\n\n$$\n\\text{MAD} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}10}{4} = 2.5\n$$"] [17592186147181 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the mean of the data:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\text{mean}} = \\dfrac{3 + 1 + 4 + 8}{4} = \\dfrac{16}{4} = {\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the mean from each number in the data set, find the absolute values of the results, and add all these together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3-{\\color{blue}{4}}|+|1-{\\color{blue}{4}}|+|4-{\\color{blue}{4}}|+|8-{\\color{blue}{4}}| &= |-1|+|-3|+|0|+|4| \\\\ &= 1+3+0+4 \\\\ &= {\\color{red}{8}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the mean absolute deviation (MAD), we divide the sum obtained above (${\\color{red}8}$) by the total number of data points ($4$):\n\n$$\n\\text{MAD} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}8}{4} = 2\n$$"] [17592186147182 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the mean of the data:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\text{mean}} = \\dfrac{14 + 16 + 13 + 21 + 19}{5} = \\dfrac{83}{5} = {\\color{blue}16.6}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the mean from each number in the data set, find the absolute values of the results, and add all these together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|14-{\\color{blue}{16.6}}|+|16-{\\color{blue}{16.6}}|+|13-{\\color{blue}{16.6}}|+|21-{\\color{blue}{16.6}}|+|19-{\\color{blue}{16.6}}| &= |-2.6|+|-0.6|+|-3.6|+|4.4|+|2.4| \\\\ &= 2.6+0.6+3.6+4.4+2.4 \\\\ &= {\\color{red}{13.6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the mean absolute deviation (MAD), we divide the sum obtained above (${\\color{red}13.6}$) by the total number of data points ($5$):\n\n$$\n\\text{MAD} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}13.6}{5} = 2.72\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest tenth gives:\n$2.72 \\rightarrow 2.7$\n\nTherefore, the mean absolute deviation of the times of the racers is $2.7$ minutes."] [17592186147219 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal L \\{f(t)\\} = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s \\neq 0$ or $s = 0.$\n\n- If $s \\neq 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =2\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{s}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{a\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{e}^{-sa}=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & s>0, \\\\ \\mathrm{\\infty },\\hspace{1em} & s<0.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s = 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =2\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =2\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =2\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s = 0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t\n$$ is divergent\n\n- For $s > 0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t\n$$ is convergent\n\n- For $s < 0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t\n$$ is divergent\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s > 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-\\frac{2}{s}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{s}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)=2$ is $F(s) =\\dfrac{2}{s},$ defined for $s > 0.$"] [17592186147220 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal L \\{f(t)\\} = \n\\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)=e^{-2t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-2t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st-2t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-2-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-2-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-2-s)t}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s \\neq -2$ or $s = -2.$\n\n- If $s \\neq -2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-2-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{-2-s}{e}^{(-2-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2-s}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-2-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}(1-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-2-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}-\\frac{1}{s+2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-2-s)a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{a\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{e}^{(-2-s)a}=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & s>-2, \\\\ \\mathrm{\\infty },\\hspace{1em} & s<-2.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s = -2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-2) & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-2-(-2))t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s \\leq -2$ the integral is divergent.\n\n- For $s > -2$ the integral is convergent.\n\nEvaluating for $s > -2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+2}-\\frac{1}{s+2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-2-s)a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}-\\frac{1}{s+2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Laplace transform of $f(t)=e^{-2t}$ is $F(s) =\\dfrac{1}{s+2},$ defined for $s > -2.$"] [17592186147221 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the function $f(t)=e^{2\\textrm i t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{e}^{2\\text{i}t}\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(2\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating in the usual way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(2\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(2\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}-s}{e}^{(2\\text{i}-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}-s}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(2\\text{i}-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-2\\text{i}}(1-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(2\\text{i}-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-2\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s-2\\text{i}}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot {e}^{2\\text{i}a}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall Euler's formula:\n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm i\\theta} = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm i \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nF(s)= \\dfrac{1}{s-2\\textrm i} -  \\dfrac{1}{s-2\\textrm i}\\left[\\lim_{a\\to \\infty}e^{ -sa}\\cdot \\left(\\cos(2a) + \\textrm i\\sin(2a)\\right)  \\right].\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following cases:\n\n- For $s < 0,$ the term $e^{-sa}$ increases without bound while $\\cos(2a) + \\textrm i\\sin(2a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to\\infty.$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s < 0.$\n\n- For $s = 0,$ we have simply $\\cos(2a) + \\textrm i\\sin(2a),$ which oscillates rapidly as $a\\to\\infty.$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s = 0.$\n\n- For $s > 0,$ the term $e^{-sa}$ decays to zero while $\\cos(2a) + \\textrm i\\sin(2a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to\\infty.$ Therefore, the limit of their product equals zero.\n\nTherefore, for $s > 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s-2\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s-2\\text{i}}[\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2a))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-2\\text{i}}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-2\\text{i}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $e^{2\\textrm i t}$ is $F(s) =\\dfrac{1}{s-2\\textrm i},$ defined for $s>0.$"] [17592186147270 :question/worked-solution "Let's go through the possibilities one at a time.\n\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}0}$ into the given inequality, we get:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\color{blue}{x}} & <10 \\\\ 2\\times {\\color{blue}{x}} & <10 \\\\ 2\\times {\\color{blue}{0}} & <10 \\\\ 0 & <10\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is a *true* statement, so $x=0$ *satisfies* this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}2}$ into the given inequality, we get:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\color{blue}{x}} & <10 \\\\ 2\\times {\\color{blue}{x}} & <10 \\\\ 2\\times {\\color{blue}{2}} & <10 \\\\ 4 & <10\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is a *true* statement, so $x=2$ *satisfies* this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}5}$ into the given inequality, we get:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\color{blue}{x}} & <10 \\\\ 2\\times {\\color{blue}{x}} & <10 \\\\ 2\\times {\\color{blue}{5}} & <10 \\\\ 10 & <10\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is a *false* statement, so $x=5$ *does not satisfy* this inequality.\n\nTherefore, only I and II satisfy the inequality."] [17592186147271 :question/worked-solution "Let's go through the possibilities one at a time.\n\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}a} = {\\color{blue}2}$ into the inequality I, we get:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}{a}}+2 & >5 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{2}}+2 & >5 \\\\ 4 & >5\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is a *false* statement, so $a = 2$ *does not satisfy* this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}a}={\\color{blue}2}$ into the inequality II, we get:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\color{blue}{a}} & <6 \\\\ 2\\times {\\color{blue}{a}} & <6 \\\\ 2\\times {\\color{blue}{2}} & <6 \\\\ 4 & <6\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is a *true* statement, so $a = 2$ *satisfies* this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}a}={\\color{blue}2}$ into the inequality III, we get:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\color{blue}{a}}-1 & <10 \\\\ 3\\times {\\color{blue}{a}}-1 & <10 \\\\ 3\\times {\\color{blue}{2}}-1 & <10 \\\\ 5 & <10\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is a *true* statement, so $a = 2$ *satisfies* this inequality.\n\nTherefore, the value $a=2$ only satisfies inequalities II and III."] [17592186147272 :question/worked-solution "Let's go through the possibilities one at a time.\n\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}t}={\\color{blue}9}$ into the given inequality, we get:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{\\color{blue}{t}}}{3} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{2\\times {\\color{blue}{t}}}{3} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{2\\times {\\color{blue}{9}}}{3} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{18}{3} & <8 \\\\ 6 & <8\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is a *true* statement, so $t=9$ *satisfies* this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}t}={\\color{blue}6}$ into the given inequality, we get:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{\\color{blue}{t}}}{3} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{2\\times {\\color{blue}{t}}}{3} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{2\\times {\\color{blue}{6}}}{3} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{12}{3} & <8 \\\\ 4 & <8\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is a *true* statement, so $t=6$ *satisfies* this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}t}={\\color{blue}3}$ into the given inequality, we get:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{\\color{blue}{t}}}{3} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{2\\times {\\color{blue}{t}}}{3} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{2\\times {\\color{blue}{3}}}{3} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{6}{3} & <8 \\\\ 2 & <8\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is a *true* statement, so $t=3$ *satisfies* this inequality.\n\nTherefore, I, II, and III all satisfy the inequality."] [17592186147273 :question/worked-solution "Let's go through the possibilities one at a time.\n\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}z}={\\color{blue}1}$ into inequality I, we get:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\color{blue}{z}}+4 & <8 \\\\ 5\\times {\\color{blue}{z}}+4 & <8 \\\\ 5\\times {\\color{blue}{1}}+4 & <8 \\\\ 9 & <8\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is a *false* statement, so $z = 1$ *does not satisfy* this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}z}={\\color{blue}1}$ into inequality II, we get:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}{z}}+\\frac{1}{3} & <\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{1}}+\\frac{1}{3} & <\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1\\times 3+1}{3} & <\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3} & <\\frac{2}{3}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is a *false* statement, so $z = 1$ *does not satisfy* this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}z}={\\color{blue}1}$ into inequality III, we get:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\color{blue}{z}}}{2} & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{\\color{blue}{1}}}{2} & <1\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is a *true* statement, so $z = 1$ *satisfies* this inequality.\n\nTherefore, the value $z=1$ only satisfies inequality III."] [17592186147320 :question/worked-solution "The dot plot tells us the following:\n\n- $1$ member practiced for $3$ hours\n- $1$ member practiced for $4$ hours\n- $1$ member practiced for $5$ hours\n- $2$ members practiced for $6$ hours\n- $1$ member practiced for $9$ hours\n\nSo, we get the following data:\n\n$$\n3, \\: 4, \\: 5, \\: 6, \\: 6, \\: 9\n$$\n\nNow, we find the mean of the data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmean &= ((3 \\cdot 1) + (4 \\cdot 1) + (5 \\cdot 1) + (6 \\cdot 2) + (9 \\cdot 1))/(6) \\\\\n&= \\frac{33}{6} \\\\\n&= 5.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the mean from each number in the data set, find the absolute value of the results, and add all these together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3-{\\color{blue}{5.5}}|+|4-{\\color{blue}{5.5}}|+|5-{\\color{blue}{5.5}}|+|6-{\\color{blue}{5.5}}|+|6-{\\color{blue}{5.5}}|+|9-{\\color{blue}{5.5}}| &= |-2.5|+|-1.5|+|-0.5|+|\\hspace{0.167em}0.5\\hspace{0.167em}|+|\\hspace{0.167em}0.5\\hspace{0.167em}|+|\\hspace{0.167em}3.5\\hspace{0.167em}| \\\\ &= 2.5+1.5+0.5+0.5+0.5+3.5 \\\\ &= {\\color{red}{9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the mean absolute deviation (MAD), we divide the sum obtained above (${\\color{red}9}$) by the total number of data points ($6$). This gives\n\n$$\n\\text{MAD} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}9}{6} = 1.5\n$$"] [17592186148710 :tutorial/content "We can find which values satisfy an inequality by substituting a number for the variable and checking whether the result is true.\n\nFor example, consider the following inequality\n\n$$\ny - 1 > 3\n$$\n\nWhich of the following values of $y$ satisfies the inequality?\n\n- $y=6$\n- $y=8$\n- $y=3$\n\nLet's go through the possibilities one by one.\n\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}y}={\\color{blue}6}$ into the given inequality, we get:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}{y}}-1 & >3 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{6}}-1 & >3 \\\\ 5 & >3\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is a *true* statement, so $y=6$ *satisfies* this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}y}={\\color{blue}8}$ into the given inequality, we get:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}{y}}-1 & >3 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{8}}-1 & >3 \\\\ 7 & >3\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is a *true* statement, so $y=8$ *satisfies* this inequality.\n\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}y}={\\color{blue}3}$ into the given inequality, we get:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}{y}}-1 & >3 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{3}}-1 & >3 \\\\ 2 & >3\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is a *false* statement, so $y=3$ *does not satisfy* this inequality.\n\nTherefore, the values that satisfy the inequality are $y = 6$ and $y = 8$."] [17592186148763 :tutorial/content "Assume that $f(t)$ is continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ and of exponential order $s_0$, and that\n$f'(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$.\n\nThe Laplace transform of $f$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n= \\int_{0}^{\\infty} e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nReplacing $f(t)$ with $f'(t)$ in this definition gives\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f'(t)\\}\n= \\int_{0}^{\\infty} e^{-st} f'(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWriting the improper integral as a limit of proper integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{'}(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{f}^{'}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}{f}^{'}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply integration by parts on $[0,a]$, choosing\n\n$$\nu = e^{-st},\n\\qquad\n\\textrm{d}v = f'(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\textrm{d}u = -s e^{-st}\\,\\textrm{d}t,\n\\qquad\nv = f(t).\n$$\n\nUsing the integration-by-parts formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}{f}^{'}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={e}^{-st}f(t){|}_{0}^{a}-{\\int }_{0}^{a}(-s{e}^{-st})f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={e}^{-sa}f(a)-f(0)+s{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the previous expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{'}(t)} & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-sa}f(a)-f(0)+s{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =s{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-f(0)+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}f(a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is of exponential order $s_0$, there exist constants $K>0$ and $T>0$ such that\n\n$$\n|f(t)| \\le K e^{s_0 t}\n\\qquad \\text{for all } t>T.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $s>s_0$,\n\n$$\n|e^{-sa} f(a)|\n\\le K e^{-(s-s_0)a}\n\\longrightarrow 0\n\\quad \\text{as } a\\to\\infty.\n$$\n\nThus, we have established the **derivative property**:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f'(t)\\}\n= s\\,\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} - f(0),\n\\qquad s>s_0.\n$$"] [17592186151988 :question/worked-solution "To decipher a message encrypted using the linear cipher with key $a=15$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the ciphertext in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Find the inverse to $a=15$ modulo $26$. To do that, we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{ccccc}26 & = & 15\\cdot 1 & + & 11 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 15 & = & 11\\cdot 1 & + & 4 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 11 & = & 4\\cdot 2 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 4 & = & 3\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle 11|= 26 - 15; 4|= 15 - 11; 3|= 11 - 4 \\cdot 2; 1|= 4 - 3\n$$\nThen, we back-substitute:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 1|= 4 - 3; = 4 - (11 - 4 \\cdot 2); = 4 \\cdot 3 - 11; = (15 - 11) \\cdot 3 - 11; = 15 \\cdot 3 - 11 \\cdot 4; = 15 \\cdot 3 - (26 - 15) \\cdot 4; = 15 \\cdot 7 - 26 \\cdot 4; = 15 \\cdot 7 + 26 \\cdot (-4)\n$$\nWe can write this result in modulo $26$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}15 \\cdot 7 + 26 \\cdot (-4) & ≡1 & (mod26) \\\\ 15 \\cdot 7 & ≡1 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nSo we have $15 \\cdot 7 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:26)$, which means that the inverse of $a=15$ modulo $26$ is $a^{-1}=7$.\n- Multiply each index by $a^{-1}=7$ modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\cdot 25≡175≡19 & (mod26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 0≡0≡0 & (mod26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 9≡63≡11 & (mod26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 20≡140≡10 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the answer is $\\text{TALK}$."] [17592186152013 :question/worked-solution "The special case of Fermat's little theorem states the following:\n\n> $a^{p-1} \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:p)$ for any prime $p$ and any integer $a$ that is coprime with $p$.\n\nFirst, notice that $7$ is prime and\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(4,7)=1\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the special case of Fermat's little theorem:\n\n$$\n4^{7-1} \\equiv {\\color{blue}4^{6}} \\equiv {\\color{blue}1} \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:7)\n$$\n\nNow, re-writing the given exponent, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{80} & ≡{4}^{13\\cdot 6+2} &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}7) \\\\  & ≡{({\\color{blue}{{4}^{6}}})}^{13}\\cdot {4}^{2} &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}7) \\\\  & ≡{({\\color{blue}{1}})}^{13}\\cdot 16 &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}7) \\\\  & ≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}7)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186152015 :question/worked-solution "The special case of Fermat's little theorem states the following:\n\n> $a^{p-1} \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:p)$ for any prime $p$ and any integer $a$ that is coprime with $p$.\n\nFirst, notice that $13$ is prime and\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(4,13)=1\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the special case of Fermat's little theorem:\n\n$$\n4^{13-1} \\equiv 4^{\\color{blue}12} \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:13)\n$$\n\nLet's now find $7^{100}$ modulo ${\\color{blue}12}$. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 7^{1}\\begin{vmatrix}≡7 & (mod12) \\\\ 7^{2} & ≡1\\end{vmatrix}(mod12); 7^{3}\\begin{vmatrix}≡7 & (mod12) \\\\ 7^{4} & ≡1\\end{vmatrix}(mod12); ⋮\n$$\nThis pattern will continue. So, $7$ to the power of any even integer (like $100$) will give us $1$ modulo $12$, and we can write\n\n$$\n7^{100} = 12k+1\n$$\n\nfor some integer $k$.\n\nNow, re-writing the given exponent, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{{7}^{100}} & ≡{4}^{12k+1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}13) \\\\  & ≡{({4}^{12})}^{k}\\cdot {4}^{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}13) \\\\  & ≡{(1)}^{k}\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}13) \\\\  & ≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}13)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186152018 :question/worked-solution "Euler's theorem states that if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, then\n\n$$\na^{\\phi(n)} \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:n)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\phi$ is Euler's totient function.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(7,33)=1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nϕ(33) &= ϕ(3) \\cdot ϕ(11) \\\\\n&= (3 - 1) \\cdot (11 - 1) \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply Euler's theorem:\n\n$$\n7^{\\phi(33)} \\equiv {\\color{blue}7^{20}} \\equiv {\\color{blue}1} \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:33)\n$$\n\nNow, rewriting the given exponent, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{122} & ≡{7}^{20\\cdot 6+2} &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}33) \\\\  & ≡{({\\color{blue}{{7}^{20}}})}^{6}\\cdot {7}^{2} &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}33) \\\\  & ≡{({\\color{blue}{1}})}^{6}\\cdot 49 &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}33) \\\\  & ≡16 &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}33)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186152019 :question/worked-solution "Euler's theorem states that if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, then\n\n$$\na^{\\phi(n)} \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:n)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\phi$ is Euler's totient function.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(4,21)=1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nϕ(21) &= ϕ(3) \\cdot ϕ(7) \\\\\n&= (3 - 1) \\cdot (7 - 1) \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply Euler's theorem:\n\n$$\n4^{\\phi(21)} \\equiv 4^{\\color{blue}12} \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:21)\n$$\n\nLet's now find $5^{220}$ modulo ${\\color{blue}12}$. First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(5,12)=1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nϕ(12) &= ϕ(2^{2}) \\cdot ϕ(3) \\\\\n&= (4 - 2) \\cdot (3 - 1) \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply Euler's theorem:\n\n$$\n5^{\\phi(12)} \\equiv 5^{\\color{red}4} \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:12)\n$$\n\nNow, re-writing $5^{220}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{220} & ≡{5}^{4\\cdot 55} &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}12) \\\\  & ≡{({5}^{{\\color{red}{4}}})}^{55} &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}12) \\\\  & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that\n\n$$\n5^{220} = 12k + 1\n$$\n\nfor some integer $k$.\n\nFinally, re-writing the given exponent, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{{5}^{220}} & ≡{4}^{12k+1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}21) \\\\  & ≡{({4}^{12})}^{k}\\cdot {4}^{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}21) \\\\  & ≡{(1)}^{k}\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}21) \\\\  & ≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}21)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186152037 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne = \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{n}\\right)^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{3n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{3} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186152038 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne^x = \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{x}{n}\\right)^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{-n/2} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}]}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}]}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ={({e}^{x})}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186152039 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne = \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{n}\\right)^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{nx/3} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{x/3} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{x/3} \\\\  & ={e}^{x/3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{{e}^{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186152386 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(1 + \\frac{1}{n})^{-2n}$?"] [17592186152387 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(1 + \\frac{x}{n})^{-3n}$?"] [17592186152388 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(1 + \\frac{1}{n})^{4nx}$?"] [17592186152389 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(1 + \\frac{x}{n})^{2nx}$?"] [17592186152390 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(1 + \\frac{1}{n})^{n/2}$?"] [17592186152391 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{nx/2} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{x/2} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{x/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186152825 :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed between the curves $y={x}^{2}-6$ and $y=2-{x}^{2}.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186152825 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/76/760595b548b2327b9c7c44f336d2c0c2b6a69688c887587f162f8ff2ac5bb5ab.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-6,6]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-6,6]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-6,6]$” only."] [17592186152827 :question/problem "![](images/d7/d7db51d6af763e8853f815feb6e74b88d0ca9a19e79c42b3a42da4e8b088f93c.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}x+y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{cases}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186152827 :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}x+y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,1]$ and $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$ do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$ only."] [17592186152828 :question/problem "![](images/e8/e8392135cf2ffbfd91d51fee015cc4ddc6778b890e40e1f63eae18b6927e85e8.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186152828 :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-6,6]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,2]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,4]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,4]$” only."] [17592186153326 :tutorial/content "The **unit step function**, $u(t)$, is used to model quantities that change suddenly at a specific time, such as the application of a force, the switching of a circuit, or the start of a signal.\n\nThe unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}1, & t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & t<0.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the function is *off* before $t=0$ and *on* afterward. The graph of the unit step function is shown below.\n\n![](images/66/666fe470145e36e88a33d0719268c25226606d4de10e57f1a8e97ccfa4d34eaa.png)\n\nThere are two primary ways to modify the unit step function:\n\n- **Scaling**: Multiplying $u(t)$ by a constant changes the magnitude (height) of the step.\n\n- **Shifting**: Replacing $t$ with $t-a$ changes the turn-on time from $t=0$ to $t=a.$ Applying only the shift results in the function $u(t - a),$ defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}0, & t<a, \\\\ 1, & t\\ge a.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThis function remains at $0$ until $t=a$ and then immediately jumps to $1.$\n\nCombining both modifications gives the general form of a **scaled and shifted** step function:\n\n$$\nf(t) = c \\cdot u(t-a)\n$$\n\nThis function remains at $0$ for $t < a$, and then jumps to the constant value $c$ at $t=a.$ In this way, the unit step function allows us to model both the *timing* and the *magnitude* of a sudden change.\n\nNext, we will look at a concrete example of graphing a specific scaled and shifted step function."] [17592186153388 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we encrypted a message using the linear cipher with key $a=3$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters. The resulting encrypted message was $\\textrm{VMU}$.\n\nTo decrypt this message, all we have to do is multiply by the inverse of the key:\n\n$$\na^{-1} \\cdot i_\\textrm{cipher} \\equiv i_\\textrm{plain} \\: (\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nLet's recall the indices of the English alphabet and then decipher the message.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">F</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">P</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">W</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">X</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo decipher a message encrypted using the linear cipher with key $a=3$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the ciphertext in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Find the inverse of $a=3$ modulo $26$. To do that, we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{ccccc}26 & = & 3\\cdot 8 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 3 & = & 2\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle 2|= 26 - 3 \\cdot 8; 1|= 3 - 2 \\cdot 1\n$$\nThen, we back-substitute:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 1|= 3 - 2; = 3 - (26 - 3 \\cdot 8); = 3 \\cdot 9 - 26; = 3 \\cdot 9 + 26 \\cdot (-1)\n$$\nWe can write this result in modulo $26$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\cdot 9 + 26 \\cdot (-1) & ≡1 & (mod26) \\\\ 3 \\cdot 9 & ≡1 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nSo we have $3 \\cdot 9 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:26)$, which means that the inverse of $a=3$ modulo $26$ is $a^{-1}=9$.\n- Multiply each index by $a^{-1}=9$ modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\cdot 21≡189≡7 & (mod26) \\\\ 9 \\cdot 12≡108≡4 & (mod26) \\\\ 9 \\cdot 20≡180≡24 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}24$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nWe successfully recovered the original message, which was $\\text{HEY}$."] [17592186153397 :tutorial/content "**Fermat's little theorem** states the following:\n\n> *If $p$ is a prime number, then $a^{p} \\equiv a \\: (\\text{mod}\\:p)$ for any integer $a$.*\n\nWe also have the following special case of the theorem:\n\n> *$a^{p-1} \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:p)$ for any prime $p$ and any integer $a$ that is coprime with $p$, i.e. $\\textrm{gcd}(a,p)=1$.*\n\nFermat's little theorem is useful because it allows us to quickly compute powers of integers modulo a prime number $p$.\n\nFor example, let's compute $5^{32} \\: \\text{mod} \\: 7$ using the special case of Fermat's little theorem. First, notice that since $7$ is prime and\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(5,7)=1\n$$\n\nwe can indeed apply the theorem. So, we get\n\n$$\n5^{7-1} \\equiv {\\color{blue}5^{6}} \\equiv {\\color{blue}1} \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:7)\n$$\n\nSo, to compute $5^{32} \\: \\text{mod} \\: 7$, we rewrite the exponent $32$ and then manipulate it using the laws of exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{32} & ≡{5}^{6\\cdot 5+2} &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}7) \\\\  & ≡({\\color{blue}{{5}^{6}}})^{5}\\cdot {5}^{2} &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}7) \\\\  & ≡({\\color{blue}{1}})^{5}\\cdot 25 &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}7) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 25 &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}7) \\\\  & ≡25 &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}7) \\\\  & ≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** A justification of the special case of Fermat's little theorem will be given at the end of this lesson. But first, let's get some practice using Fermat's little theorem and its special case."] [17592186153400 :tutorial/content "If $a$ and $n$ are coprime, then **Euler's theorem** states that\n\n$$\na^{\\phi(n)} \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:n)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\phi$ is Euler's totient function (which returns the number of positive integers that are less than $n$ and are coprime to $n$).\n\nUsing Euler's theorem, we can reduce a calculation containing large exponents to one containing smaller exponents. For example, suppose that we want to compute the following:\n\n$$\n2^{91} \\: \\text{mod} \\: 15\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(2,15)=1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nϕ(15) &= ϕ(3) \\cdot ϕ(5) \\\\\n&= (3 - 1) \\cdot (5 - 1) \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply Euler's theorem, which gives\n\n$$\n2^{\\phi(15)} \\equiv {\\color{blue}2^{8}} \\equiv {\\color{blue}1} \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:15)\\,\n$$\n\nNow, rewriting the given exponent, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{91} & ≡{2}^{8\\cdot 11+3} &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}15) \\\\  & ≡{({\\color{blue}{{2}^{8}}})}^{11}\\cdot {2}^{3} &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}15) \\\\  & ≡{({\\color{blue}{1}})}^{11}\\cdot 8 &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}15) \\\\  & ≡8 &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}15)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186153414 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the limit\n\n$$\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{x}{n}\\right)^{n},$$\n\nwhere $x$ is a real number.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n/x})}^{n} \\\\ & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n/x})}^{(n/x)\\cdot x} \\\\ & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n/x})}^{n/x}]}^{x}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nLet's now perform the substitution $m=n/x.$ Since $m\\to\\infty$ as $n\\to\\infty,$ we obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n/x})}^{n/x}]}^{x} & =[\\,\\underbrace{\\lim_{m\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{m})}^{m}}_{e}\\,]^{x} \\\\ & ={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}$$"] [17592186155332 :answer/value "|f(x) | \\ge K | g(x)|"] [17592186155333 :answer/value "|f(x) | \\le K | g(x)|"] [17592186155337 :answer/value "|f(x) | \\le K | g(x)|"] [17592186155338 :answer/value "|f(x) | \\ge K | g(x)|"] [17592186157031 :question/worked-solution "To decrypt a message using the RSA cipher, we need to know its corresponding private key.\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nn=33 = 3 \\times 11\n$$\n\nLet's denote $p=3$ and $q=11$. Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nϕ(n) &= (p - 1)(q - 1) \\\\\n&= (3 - 1)(11 - 1) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 10 \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the RSA cipher with the private key $(d,n)$, the first component $d$ is the multiplicative inverse of $e=7$ (the first component of the public key) modulo\n\n$$\n\\phi(n)=20{:}\n$$\n\n$$\nd = e^{-1} \\quad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 20)\n$$\n\nSo, let's find the inverse of $e=7$ modulo $\\phi(n)=20$. To do that, we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{ccccc}20 & = & 7\\cdot 2 & + & 6 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 7 & = & 6\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 &= 20 - 7 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n1 &= 7 - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 7 - 6 \\\\\n&= 7 - (20 - 7 \\cdot 2) \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 3 - 20 \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 3 + 20 \\cdot (-1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $20$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\cdot 3 + 20 \\cdot (-1) & ≡1 & (mod20) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 3 & ≡1 & (mod20)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nSo we have $7 \\cdot 3 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:20)$, which means that the inverse of $e=7$ modulo $20$ is $e^{-1}=3$.\n\nThus, our private key is\n\n$$\n(d,n) = (3,33)\n$$\n\nNow, to decrypt a word using the RSA cipher with the private key\n\n$$\n(d,n)=(3,33)\n$$\n\nover the English alphabet, we do the following:\n\n- Raise each index to the power of $d=3$ modulo $n=33$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}15^{3}≡09 & (mod33) \\\\ 16^{3}≡04 & (mod33) \\\\ 13^{3}≡19 & (mod33)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indexes:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}09$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}04$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}19$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the plaintext was\n$J \\:\\: E \\:\\: T$."] [17592186157125 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the set of solutions in the non-negative integers with $x > 9$ and let $B$ be the set of solutions in the non-negative integers with\n\n$$\ny \\geq 7\n$$\n\nThen the number of solutions where $x > 9$ or\n\n$$\ny \\geq 7\n$$\n\ncorresponds to $\\mid A \\cup B \\mid$, and we can compute this using the inclusion-exclusion principle:\n\n$$\n|A\\cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A\\cap B|\n$$\n\n- To compute $\\mid A \\mid$, we need to count the number of solutions in the non-negative integers such that $x > 9$, or equivalently, $x \\geq 10$. To do this, we can replace $x=10+x'$, where $x'$ is a non-negative integer, which gives\n$$\n\\displaystyle x + y + z|= 16; (10 + x^{′}) + y + z|= 16; x^{′} + y + z|= 6.\n$$\nSo, the number of solutions in $A$ is equal to the number of non-negative integer solutions of the equation above, which is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid A \\mid |= \\left(\\frac{6 + 3 - 1}{6}\\right) = \\frac{8!}{6!2!} = 28.\n$$\n- To compute $\\mid B \\mid$, we apply the same procedure. We replace $y=7+y'$ to get the equation\n$$\n\\displaystyle x + y + z|= 16; x + (7 + y^{′}) + z|= 16; x + y^{′} + z|= 9,\n$$\nand the number of solutions of this equation is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid B \\mid |= \\left(\\frac{9 + 3 - 1}{9}\\right) = \\frac{11!}{9!2!} = 55.\n$$\n- To compute $\\mid A\\cap B \\mid$, we need to find the number of solutions in the non-negative integers such that both $x > 9$ and $y \\geq 7$. So, we replace both $x=10+x'$ and $y=7+y'$ and get\n$$\n\\displaystyle x + y + z|= 16; (10 + x^{′}) + (7 + y^{′}) + z|= 16; x^{′} + y^{′} + z|=-1,\n$$\nwhich permits no non-negative integer solutions. This tells us that $\\mid A \\cap B \\mid = 0$.\n\nFinally, according to the inclusion-exclusion principle, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A\\cup B| & =|A|+|B|-|A\\cap B| \\\\  & =28+55-0 \\\\  & =83.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $83$ solutions in the non-negative integers with $x > 9$ or\n\n$$\ny \\geq 7\n$$"] [17592186157126 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ be the set of solutions of\n\n$$\nx + y + z = 17\n$$\n\nin the non-negative integers (without restriction). The number of solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle | X | & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{17 + 3 - 1}{17} \\right) = {\\displaystyle \\frac{19 !}{17 ! 2 !}} = 171.\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nHowever, we only want to count the solutions in the non-negative integers with\n\n$$\nx \\leq 10\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny \\leq 12\n$$\n\nSo, we need to subtract the number of solutions where $x > 10$ or $y > 12$.\n\nLet $A$ be the set of solutions in the non-negative integers with $x > 10$ and let $B$ be the set of solutions in the non-negative integers with $y > 12$. Then the number of solutions where $x > 10$ or $y > 12$ corresponds to $\\mid A \\cup B \\mid$, and we can compute this using the inclusion-exclusion principle:\n\n$$\n|A\\cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A\\cap B|\n$$\n\n- To compute $\\mid A \\mid$, we need to count the number of solutions in the non-negative integers such that $x > 10$, or equivalently, $x \\geq 11$. To do this, we can replace $x=11+x'$, where $x'$ is a non-negative integer, which gives\n$$\n\\displaystyle x + y + z|= 17; (11 + x^{′}) + y + z|= 17; x^{′} + y + z|= 6.\n$$\nSo, the number of solutions in $A$ is equal to the number of non-negative integer solutions of the equation above, which is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid A \\mid |= \\left(\\frac{6 + 3 - 1}{6}\\right) = \\frac{8!}{6!2!} = 28.\n$$\n- To compute $\\mid B \\mid$, we apply the same procedure. We replace $y=13+y'$ to get the equation\n$$\n\\displaystyle x + y + z|= 17; x + (13 + y^{′}) + z|= 17; x + y^{′} + z|= 4,\n$$\nand the number of solutions of this equation is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid B \\mid |= \\left(\\frac{4 + 3 - 1}{4}\\right) = \\frac{6!}{4!2!} = 15.\n$$\n- To compute $\\mid A\\cap B \\mid$, we need to find the number of solutions in the non-negative integers such that both $x > 10$ and $y > 12$. So, we replace both $x=11+x'$ and $y=13+y'$ and get\n$$\n\\displaystyle x + y + z|= 17; (11 + x^{′}) + (13 + y^{′}) + z|= 17; x^{′} + y^{′} + z|=-7,\n$$\nwhich permits no non-negative integer solutions. This tells us that $\\mid A \\cap B \\mid = 0$.\n\nFinally, according to the inclusion-exclusion principle, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A\\cup B| & =|A|+|B|-|A\\cap B| \\\\  & =28+15-0 \\\\  & =43,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the number of solutions in the non-negative integers with\n\n$$\nx \\leq 10\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny \\leq 12\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n|X|-|A\\cup B|=171 - 43 =128.\n$$"] [17592186157166 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mathcal O(g(x)) \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty.$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to a} \\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = e^x + x\\ln x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x) = x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty},$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{1} \\\\  & =1+\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =1+\\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f \\neq \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x) =\\ln x.$  Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{1/x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }x({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f \\neq \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x) = xe^{x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x}+\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0+\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1/x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f = \\mathcal O(xe^x)$ as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [17592186157215 :question/worked-solution "First, we write $28$ as a sum of powers of $2{:}$\n\n$$\n28 = 2^4+2^3 +2^2 = 16 + 8 + 4\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n8^{28} = 8^{16 +8 +4} = 8^{16} \\cdot 8^{8} \\cdot 8^4\n$$\n\nNow, we can take the corresponding values from the given table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$a$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$32$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$8^a \\: \\text{mod} \\: 67$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$64$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{14}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{62}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{28} & ≡{8}^{16}\\cdot {8}^{8}\\cdot {8}^{4} & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}67) \\\\  & ≡62\\cdot 14\\cdot 9 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}67) \\\\  & ≡7812 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}67) \\\\  & ≡116\\cdot 67+40 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}67) \\\\  & ≡40 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}67) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186157216 :question/worked-solution "First, we write $22$ as a sum of powers of $2{:}$\n\n$$\n22 = 2^4+2^2+2^1 = 16 + 4 + 2\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n9^{22} = 9^{16 + 4 + 2} =9^{16} \\cdot 9^{4} \\cdot 9^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the exponents modulo $37$ using consecutive squaring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9^{2} & ≡81≡7 & (mod37) \\\\ 9^{4} & ≡7^{2}≡49≡12 & (mod37) \\\\ 9^{8} & ≡12^{2}≡144≡33 & (mod37) \\\\ 9^{16} & ≡33^{2}≡1089≡16 & (mod37) \\\\ 9^{32} & ≡16^{2}≡256≡34 & (mod37)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, by multiplying the obtained exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{22} & ≡{9}^{16}\\cdot {9}^{4}\\cdot {9}^{2} & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}37) \\\\  & ≡16\\cdot 12\\cdot 7 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}37) \\\\  & ≡1\\hspace{0.167em}344 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}37) \\\\  & ≡36\\cdot 37+12 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}37) \\\\  & ≡12 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}37) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186157217 :question/worked-solution "For a Diffie-Hellman key exchange protocol with public generator $g$ and prime modulus $p$ between persons $A$ and $B$ with secret keys $a$ and $b$, respectively, we have the following:\n\n- Person $A$'s public key is given by\n$A \\equiv g^a \\: (\\text{mod} \\: p)$.\n- Person $B$'s public key is given by\n$B \\equiv g^b \\: (\\text{mod} \\: p)$.\n- The common shared secret is given by\n$S \\equiv A^b \\equiv B^a \\: (\\text{mod} \\: p)$.\n\nTherefore, Alfred's and Bernice's common shared secret is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ≡{B}^{a} & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}p) \\\\  & ≡{20}^{34} & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}37) \\\\  & ≡21 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}37) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186157753 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty .$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=x+2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f=O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x+2{e}^{2x})^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1+4{e}^{2x}}{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(1+4{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{2x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{2x}}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}+2 \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x)^{′}}{({e}^{2x})^{′}}+2 \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2x}}+2 \\\\  & =0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$f=O({e}^{3x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{3x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{3x}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{x}{{e}^{3x}}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{3x}}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{e}^{3x}}+\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }2{e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x)^{′}}{({e}^{3x})^{′}}+0 \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{3{e}^{3x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{3x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $”; “$f=O({e}^{3x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” only."] [17592186158433 :tutorial/content "Suppose $D$ is a diagonal matrix with diagonal entries $\\lambda_1, \\ldots, \\lambda_n{:}$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$D=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {\\lambda }_{n}\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nFor a scalar function $f$ with a Maclaurin series expansion, $f(D)$ is the diagonal matrix with diagonal entries $f(\\lambda_1), \\ldots, f(\\lambda_n){:}$\n\n$$\nf(D) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}f({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & f({\\lambda }_{2}) & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & f({\\lambda }_{n})\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor example, given the matrix\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand the function $f(x) = e^x,$ we have\n\n$$\ne^D\n=\nf(D)\n= \n\\begin{bmatrix}f(2) & 0 \\\\ 0 & f(1)\\end{bmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{1}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis rule works because raising a diagonal matrix to a power is equivalent to raising each diagonal entry to the same power.\n\nIndeed, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle\nf(x) = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty c_k x^k\n$$\nis the Maclaurin series of $f,$ then, by the definition of $f(D),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(D) & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}{c}_{k}{D}^{k} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}{c}_{k}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {\\lambda }_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}^{k} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}{c}_{k}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1}^{k} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}^{k} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {\\lambda }_{n}^{k}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}{c}_{k}{\\lambda }_{1}^{k} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}{c}_{k}{\\lambda }_{2}^{k} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}{c}_{k}{\\lambda }_{n}^{k}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}f({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & f({\\lambda }_{2}) & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & f({\\lambda }_{n})\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186158452 :tutorial/content "Recall that in the RSA cryptosystem, we have the public key $(e,n)$ and the private key $(d,n)$, where\n\n- $p$ and $q$ are (large) primes,\n- $n=pq$\n- $\\phi(n)=(p-1)(q-1)$, and\n- $e \\cdot d \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: \\phi(n))$.\n\nThe message $M$ (with $M < n$) is encrypted by computing\n$C \\equiv M^e \\quad (\\textrm{mod} \\: n)$,\nwhereas $C$ is decrypted by computing\n$M \\equiv C^d \\quad (\\textrm{mod} \\: n)$.\n\nLet's now show that after computing $C^d$ modulo $n$, we indeed restore the original message $M$.\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{d} & ≡({M}^{e})^{d} &  & \\hspace{1em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}n) \\\\  & ≡{M}^{ed} &  & \\hspace{1em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}n).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that since $e \\cdot d \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: \\phi(n))$, we have\n\n$$\ned = 1 + k \\phi(n)\n$$\n\nfor some integer $k$. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{ed} & ≡{M}^{1+kϕ(n)} &  & \\hspace{1em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}n) \\\\  & ≡M\\cdot ({M}^{ϕ(n)})^{k} &  & \\hspace{1em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}n).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we can assume that\n\n$$\n\\gcd(M,n)=1\n$$\n\nIndeed, the probability that $M$ and $n$ will have a common divisor greater than $1$ is extremely small when the primes $p$ and $q$ are large. Moreover, if this happens, the private key will be compromised anyway.\nTherefore, according to Euler's theorem, $M^{\\phi(n)} \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: n)$.\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M\\cdot ({M}^{ϕ(n)})^{k} & ≡M\\cdot {1}^{k} &  & \\hspace{1em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}n) \\\\  & ≡M\\cdot 1 &  & \\hspace{1em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}n) \\\\  & ≡M &  & \\hspace{1em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}n).\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
